    assessment: "A psychiatric assessment reviews eating patterns, weight history, body-image beliefs, exercise habits, any purging behaviours, physical health including menstrual history where relevant, and mood and anxiety symptoms, which frequently coexist with eating disorders. Physical health checks, including weight, vital signs and relevant blood tests, are an important part of assessment because eating disorders can have serious physical complications that need to be monitored alongside psychiatric treatment.",
    treatment: "Treatment typically combines psychotherapy specifically adapted for eating disorders, nutritional rehabilitation with dietary guidance, family involvement where appropriate — particularly for younger patients — and medical monitoring of physical health throughout treatment. Medication may help with coexisting depression or anxiety but is not usually the primary treatment for the eating disorder itself. Early treatment significantly improves outcomes, which is why acting on early warning signs rather than waiting matters.",
