    h1: "Depersonalization and Derealization: Symptoms and Treatment",
    overview: "Depersonalization involves feeling detached from your own thoughts, feelings or body, as though observing yourself from outside or moving through life on autopilot. Derealization involves the surrounding world feeling unreal, foggy or dreamlike. Brief episodes are common and not usually concerning, but when these experiences are frequent, persistent or distressing, they may indicate depersonalization-derealization disorder, a recognised and treatable condition, not a sign of 'going crazy,' which is a common and understandable fear people have about these symptoms.",
    symptoms: "People describe feeling like a robot, watching themselves from outside their body, emotional numbness, a sense that their own voice or actions don't feel like their own, or the world around them appearing flat, distant or artificial. Reality testing remains intact — people experiencing this know the feeling isn't literally true, which distinguishes it from psychosis and is often reassuring once explained.",
    causes: "Depersonalization-derealization is commonly triggered or worsened by significant stress, anxiety, panic attacks, sleep deprivation, trauma, or substance use, particularly cannabis. It can also occur as part of another condition such as an anxiety disorder, depression, or PTSD. The brain's stress response appears to trigger this detachment as a kind of protective mechanism that then persists beyond its usefulness.",
    assessment: "An assessment explores the frequency, triggers and duration of episodes, associated anxiety or panic, substance use, trauma history, and rules out neurological causes when relevant. Understanding what typically triggers or worsens episodes, such as stress, sleep loss or specific substances, helps shape an effective treatment plan.",
    treatment: "Psychotherapy, particularly approaches addressing the underlying anxiety that commonly drives depersonalization-derealization, is the primary treatment and can significantly reduce both frequency and distress. Reducing or stopping any contributing substance use, particularly cannabis, treating underlying anxiety or panic disorder, and psychoeducation about the harmless nature of the experience itself all support recovery. Medication may help when depersonalization occurs alongside a clinical anxiety or depressive disorder.",
    local: "Dr. Kushal Kharel's clinic in Kalanki, Kathmandu assesses and treats depersonalization-derealization symptoms, including identifying and addressing underlying anxiety or triggers.",
